# The R36S's OTG port as a USB device for good (package/usbrole), until a
# rule that tells a computer from an accessory is built from what the port
# reads. usbrole makes a port a host only while the USB PHY's ID pin (its
# extcon's USB-HOST cable) asks for power, and on the R36S nothing can: the
# PHY's otg-port, which reads that pin and the port's power (otg-id,
# otg-bvalid), is disabled in ROCKNIX's R36S tree (rk3326-gameconsole-r3xs.dtsi,
# &u2phy) and in the R36S's stock trees (dArkOSen-R36S's
# boot/dtb/r36s/*/rk3326-r36s-linux.dtb, at djparentx/dArkOSen-R36S
# 28f5427f7ae953ee6e813618705baf569fef0f27; the stock firmware is a host by
# default and has no gadget).
#
# A host for good (ROLE=host, 2026-11-27, for Sylver's flash drives) put the
# port's own power out with a computer on the cable too, and the computer
# never saw the R36S: no File Transfer, no SSH over the cable (Tarik's
# R36S-V21, 2026-11-28). A device never feeds anything, so a computer on the
# cable is safe, and the gadget (File Transfer, Developer Mode's link) can
# answer it; a flash drive or a Wi-Fi adapter gets no power until the rule
# comes. usbrole writes the port's facts into the kernel's log meanwhile:
# every power supply's online and uevent, the role, each extcon, the gadget
# controller's state and the USB devices on the bus, at the start and at
# each change, for that rule. The internal hub some boards have (dArkOSen's
# KERNEL/USB_Gadget_Mass_Storage.md, an R36H Pro Max) shows only as a host,
# so it is not looked for: a host's power could meet a computer's.
ROLE=device
